   "value": "### Result (cells 12–13, exact up to $\\varepsilon^8$; sector zeros exact)\n\n- After undoing $g_0\\otimes g_0$, **every nonzero sector projection $u_{x,\\nu}$ starts at the same order**, $\\varepsilon^{\\mathrm{ht}(\\nu)}$, where $\\mathrm{ht}(\\nu)$ is the number of simple-root steps from $(4,2,0)$ to $\\nu$. So it is $\\varepsilon^0$ at $(4,2,0)$, $\\varepsilon^1$ at $(4,1,1),(3,3,0)$, …, $\\varepsilon^4$ at the singlet sector $(2,2,2)$, and $\\varepsilon^8$ at $(0,2,4)$. The other projections vanish **exactly**, and nothing is lost to truncation.\n- In every sector the leading vectors span the **whole** sector. In particular they contain $V_\\mu\\otimes e_1^T$ for every $\\mu=\\nu-(2,1,0)$, including $\\mu=(0,1,2)$ in the singlet sector at $\\varepsilon^4$.\n- **Graded limit.** Rescale sector $\\nu$ by $\\varepsilon^{-\\mathrm{ht}(\\nu)}$. The 64 limit covectors $\\Lambda_x$ then have **rank 64**, so the SoV basis survives the untwisting limit as a basis in this graded sense. Hence a single $\\varepsilon$-independent combination gives any graded target. In particular it gives $(e_1^TP)\\otimes e_1^T$ with $P=\\exp(\\varepsilon aE_{12}+\\varepsilon^2bE_{13}+\\varepsilon cE_{23})$ for arbitrary $a,b,c$.\n- The grading is exactly the scaling of $G_1=g_0Gg_0^{-1}$: its $E_{12}$ and $E_{23}$ entries are $O(\\varepsilon)$. $E_{13}$ counts as two steps, $O(\\varepsilon^2)$, consistent with $[E_{12},E_{23}]=E_{13}$.\n- Not done: the explicit combination coefficients (a symbolic 64×64 solve, aborted for time), and a control run on a second untwisting curve.",
